  "body": "At the lexical level, in the BEFORE DOCTYPE NAME and DOCTYPE NAME states, where characters may be added to a doctype token’s name, upper ascii characters are normalized to lowercase as they’re added to the name, so that for example `<!DOCTYPE HtMl>` will cause a doctype token with the name \"html\" to be emitted.\r\n\r\nHowever at the syntactic level, in the [INITIAL insertion mode](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#the-initial-insertion-mode), when a doctype token is encountered, it says: \r\n\r\n> If the DOCTYPE token's name is not a case\\-sensitive match for the string \"html\" [...]\r\n\r\nIs the \"case sensitive\" part here redundant, or is there an avenue by which a doctype token can be created where it would not have its name’s ascii alpha chars lowercased?",
